---
description: UI component structure + hook-driven logic; Next.js app composes ui/* with shell-only exceptions
globs: ui/**/src/**/*.{ts,tsx},apps/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---
# UI code structure and responsibilities

## UI Code rules

- event handlers must be created before rendering. Do not add it inline in DOM
- className must be resolved before added to DOM
- split component into single responsibility.
- more smaller components are better than one big component
- any text in component use Text component
- any complex className must be resolved using function in ui-utils
- Use scss files

## File split (required)

- Keep UI code split into distinct files in a subfolder for all the component related files:
  - `types.ts`: types for the component/hook (props, state shape, options, return types)
  - `<Component>.tsx`: rendering only
  - `<Component>.module.scss`: all styles (scss modules)
  - `<Component>.stories.tsx`: Storybook stories for all supported variants/states
  - `<Component>.test.tsx`: tests colocated with the component when applicable
- All files related to a component must be added to a folder with the same name as the component

## Constants (required)

- If any constants are added to component:
  - Create a `constants.ts` file and move it all there.
- For constants consider: static text, numbers, map objects

## Component vs hook responsibilities (required)

- `<Component>.tsx` must be focused on rendering:
  - no business logic, no derived state orchestration, no side-effects
  - keep event handlers as thin delegations to hook actions
- If the component has logic, create a dedicated hook and use it from the component:
  - `use<Component>.ts` holds state, derived values, effects, memoization, callbacks
  - the hook returns a small “view model” (values + handlers) consumed by the component

## Composition and subcomponents (required)

- Component is considered as subcomponent if it's:
  - list
  - item in list
  - dom tree deeper than 3 layers
- If a component is composed of multiple meaningful parts, create subcomponents:
  - extract each part into its own `*.tsx` (and `*.module.scss` if needed)
  - subcomponents should also be render-focused; shared logic stays in hooks

## Apps package rules (required)

Apps packages under `apps/*` own routing and connect `ui/*` components to data and logic. They must not contain business logic or reusable UI components.

### Routing

- `apps/*` packages are the single place responsible for defining routes
- Use file-based routing conventions of the underlying framework (e.g. Next.js App Router)
- Route segments live in `app/` — only `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `template.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `route.ts`, `default.tsx`

### page.tsx / layout.tsx rules (required)

- Must contain **no logic** — no state, no effects, no derived values, no data fetching, no conditionals beyond trivial metadata
- Must only import and compose components; delegate all logic to hooks or server-only helpers
- Every page that requires logic must have a dedicated `use<Page>.ts` hook that holds all state, effects, and derived values; the page file only calls the hook and passes results to components

### Components in `apps/*` (required)

- Components defined inside `apps/*` are **structural connectors only**:
  - They wire `ui/*` components to API clients, hooks, or other logic packages
  - They may not define new visual language, new design primitives, or business rules
- **No new reusable components** may be created inside `apps/*` — if a component could be used outside this specific route, create a new `ui/*` package for it
- Page components must compose subcomponents; a page must never render a single monolithic template

### API calls (required)

- All API calls (GraphQL or HTTP) must use the `ui-api-client` package
- Do not import API clients, fetch wrappers, or raw `fetch` directly into components or `page.tsx` files
- API calls belong exclusively inside hooks (`use<Page>.ts` or dedicated `use<Feature>.ts`); components only receive data as props or from hooks

